@page "/help"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.AllowAnonymous]
@layout GoatLab.Client.Layout.LandingLayout

<PageTitle>Help — GoatLab</PageTitle>

<HeadContent>
    <meta name="description" content="How to use GoatLab — adding goats, listing for sale, taking reservations, vet share-links, transfers, and more." />
</HeadContent>

<section class="help-page">
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Help</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            How to do common things in GoatLab. Use the table of contents to jump around;
            each section is small enough to read in under a minute. Missing something?
            <MudLink Href="https://github.com/chrisdfennell/GoatLabSaaS/issues" Target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open an issue on GitHub</MudLink>
            and we'll add it.
        </p>

        <nav class="help-toc" aria-label="Help table of contents">
            <strong>Jump to:</strong>
            <a href="#getting-started">Getting started</a>
            <a href="#add-goats">Adding goats</a>
            <a href="#health">Health records</a>
            <a href="#breeding">Breeding &amp; kidding</a>
            <a href="#milk">Milk tracking</a>
            <a href="#listing">Listing a goat for sale</a>
            <a href="#public-profile">Public farm page</a>
            <a href="#deposits">Reservations &amp; Stripe deposits</a>
            <a href="#waitlist">Buyer waitlist</a>
            <a href="#inquiries">Buyer messages</a>
            <a href="#vet-shares">Vet share-links</a>
            <a href="#transfers">Farm-to-farm transfers</a>
            <a href="#alerts">Smart alerts &amp; push</a>
            <a href="#reports">Reports &amp; forecasts</a>
            <a href="#api">API &amp; webhooks</a>
            <a href="#self-host">Self-hosting</a>
        </nav>

        <article id="getting-started">
            <h2>Getting started</h2>
            <p>
                After signing up, you land on the <strong>dashboard</strong>. The onboarding checklist
                at the top walks you through the first few steps. The shortest path to getting value:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Set your farm name + location</strong> in <MudLink Href="/farm-settings">Farm settings</MudLink>. The location shows up on your public listings and feeds the <code>state=</code> filter on the marketplace.</li>
                <li><strong>Pick units</strong> — pounds or kilograms — same place. Affects how weights, feed, and milk display everywhere.</li>
                <li><strong>Invite helpers</strong> if anyone else logs animals: <MudLink Href="/farm-settings/members">Members</MudLink>. Each gets their own login; permissions are role-based.</li>
                <li><strong>Add your first goat</strong> (see below). Even one goat unlocks the rest of the app.</li>
            </ol>
        </article>

        <article id="add-goats">
            <h2>Adding goats</h2>
            <p>Two ways:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>One at a time:</strong> <MudLink Href="/herd/new">Herd → Add goat</MudLink>. Name, ear tag, breed, gender, date of birth. Photos and documents come later — you can save with the basics.</li>
                <li><strong>Bulk import:</strong> <MudLink Href="/tools/import">Tools → Import</MudLink>. Drag in a CSV. The importer accepts ADGA / AGS exports and a generic columns layout — column mapping happens before commit so you can fix mismatches.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Pedigree:</strong> if you enter a sire/dam by registration number that
                another GoatLab farm has marked public, the lineage page links back to that farm
                automatically. Useful for buyers verifying provenance.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="health">
            <h2>Health records</h2>
            <p>
                Every goat has a <strong>Health</strong> tab. Add medical records (vaccinations,
                dewormings, vet visits, treatments) and weights. Each record can have a
                <em>next due date</em> — that's what feeds the alerts system below.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>FAMACHA scoring</strong> for parasite load. The dashboard's "at-risk" widget pulls from these.</li>
                <li><strong>Body condition scoring</strong> for over/underweight flags.</li>
                <li><strong>Withdrawal periods</strong> on medications block sales and milk logging until the hold expires (so you don't accidentally ship a recently-treated goat or contaminated milk).</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Need to share history with a vet? See <a href="#vet-shares">vet share-links</a>.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="breeding">
            <h2>Breeding &amp; kidding</h2>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Heat detection:</strong> log heats on a doe to predict her next cycle.</li>
                <li><strong>Breeding records:</strong> pair doe + buck + date. GoatLab calculates the estimated due date (150 days for most breeds, configurable per breed).</li>
                <li><strong>Kidding records:</strong> when she kids, fill the kid count, sex, vigor, and weights. Kids can be promoted to full goat records (with the parents pre-filled) once you decide which to keep.</li>
                <li><strong>Mate recommendations:</strong> for each doe, GoatLab ranks every intact buck in your herd by projected offspring COI plus daughter production. Useful for line-breeding decisions.</li>
            </ol>
        </article>

        <article id="milk">
            <h2>Milk tracking</h2>
            <p>
                Daily milk logs per doe → trends per goat, per herd, per lactation. Test days
                (with butterfat, protein, SCC if you DHIR) live alongside the daily logs.
                Reports → Insights → Milk gives you 30 / 60 / 90-day trend charts and a
                cost-per-gallon tile if you also track feed.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="listing">
            <h2>Listing a goat for sale</h2>
            <p>Two switches matter:</p>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Enable your <a href="#public-profile">public farm page</a></strong> in Farm settings. Without it, no listing surfaces externally.</li>
                <li>On the goat's detail page, flip <strong>"List for sale"</strong> on. Set an asking price (optional but recommended — listings without a price filter to "Inquire" only and convert worse).</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                <strong>Photos sell.</strong> A listing with 5+ photos lands a buyer 3-4× more
                often than one with a single photo. Use the photo grid on the goat's detail
                page to upload multiple — the first one (or whichever you mark "primary")
                becomes the cover image on the marketplace card.
            </p>
            <p>
                Once listed, the goat appears on your <code>/pub/{your-farm}/{goat}</code> page,
                shows up in <MudLink Href="/marketplace">marketplace</MudLink> and breed search,
                and gets indexed by Google with structured data so it's eligible for rich results.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="public-profile">
            <h2>Public farm page</h2>
            <p>
                Lives at <code>/pub/{your-slug}</code>. Buyers land here from the marketplace,
                breed directory, Google, or anywhere you share the URL. To enable:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Go to <MudLink Href="/farm-settings">Farm settings</MudLink> → <strong>Public profile</strong> → flip on.</li>
                <li>Set your slug (your URL — keep it short and memorable).</li>
                <li>Optionally drop a <strong>map pin</strong> on the embedded map so buyers using map view see your location. Available on Farm and Dairy plans.</li>
                <li>Optionally enable <strong>custom branding</strong> (accent color + welcome message) — Dairy plan.</li>
            </ol>
        </article>

        <article id="deposits">
            <h2>Reservations &amp; Stripe deposits</h2>
            <p>
                Buyers can reserve a goat by paying a refundable deposit through Stripe Checkout.
                The deposit is recorded against your <a href="#waitlist">waitlist</a>; you set the
                refund policy in your farm description.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Connect Stripe:</strong> Farm settings → Billing &amp; payouts. Onboarding sends you to Stripe to verify identity and bank info.</li>
                <li><strong>Set deposit %</strong> on Farm settings. Default is 0 (no online deposits — buyers can still inquire). 10–25% is typical for goats.</li>
                <li><strong>Listing must have an asking price</strong> — the deposit is a percentage of that price.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                A buyer who clicks <strong>Reserve</strong> on a listing fills name + email,
                lands on Stripe Checkout, and on success ends up on your waitlist with the
                deposit recorded against their account.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="waitlist">
            <h2>Buyer waitlist</h2>
            <p>
                <MudLink Href="/waitlist">Buyer waitlist</MudLink> is your CRM for interested
                buyers. Each entry has breed/sex preferences, deposit amount (if any), priority,
                and notes. You can:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Add buyers manually (pre-existing customers from before GoatLab).</li>
                <li>Auto-enroll buyers from Stripe deposits (see above).</li>
                <li>Convert a waitlist entry to a sale in one click — pulls the deposit forward as a credit.</li>
                <li>Send the buyer a magic-link to a portal where they see their place in line.</li>
            </ul>
        </article>

        <article id="inquiries">
            <h2>Buyer messages</h2>
            <p>
                When a buyer fills the contact form on a listing, you get an email and the thread
                appears in <MudLink Href="/inquiries">Inquiries</MudLink>. Reply in the inbox or
                directly to the email — both surfaces stay in sync. Mark a thread "Sold" or
                "Closed" when it's resolved to keep the inbox tidy.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="vet-shares">
            <h2>Vet share-links</h2>
            <p>
                From any goat's detail page, click <strong>Share with vet</strong> to mint a
                time-bounded URL the vet can open without signing up. They see medical history,
                weights, FAMACHA, and body condition, and can leave a note that lands on the
                goat's timeline. Default expiry is 30 days; revoke any time.
                Available on Farm and Dairy plans.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="transfers">
            <h2>Farm-to-farm transfers</h2>
            <p>
                Selling a goat to another GoatLab farm? <strong>Transfer</strong> moves the
                animal — pedigree, medical history, weights, milk records, photos — to the
                buyer's herd in one click. The buyer accepts via a token link sent to their
                email. The seller's record stays as historical reference.
                Available on Farm and Dairy plans.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="alerts">
            <h2>Smart alerts &amp; push</h2>
            <p>
                <MudLink Href="/alerts">Alerts</MudLink> watches your herd for things you'd
                otherwise forget:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Overdue medications based on each record's <em>next due date</em>.</li>
                <li>Upcoming kiddings within the next 14 days.</li>
                <li>Weight drops &gt; 10% in 30 days.</li>
                <li>Low feed inventory (if you track feed).</li>
                <li>FAMACHA scores trending high.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Delivery options: in-app banner, email digest (daily 8am local), and Web Push.
                Push needs a one-time browser permission grant — install GoatLab as a PWA on
                your phone (Chrome/Safari "Add to home screen") to get them in the barn.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="reports">
            <h2>Reports &amp; forecasts</h2>
            <p>
                <MudLink Href="/reports">Reports</MudLink> has six built-in reports:
                P&amp;L, milk trends, kidding, mortality, parasite/FAMACHA, and health spend.
                Each renders as a chart + a downloadable CSV.
            </p>
            <p>
                Forecasts (Farm + Dairy) project the next 30 / 60 / 90 days for kidding load,
                expected milk volume, and cash flow based on trailing averages.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article id="api">
            <h2>API &amp; webhooks</h2>
            <p>
                Integrate GoatLab with your other tools (registries, accounting, custom dashboards):
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>API keys:</strong> Farm settings → API. Generate a key (one-shot reveal — copy it). Authenticate any endpoint with <code>Authorization: Bearer goatlab_…</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Webhooks:</strong> subscribe to events (goat created, sale closed, kidding logged, medical record). HMAC-SHA256 signatures, automatic retries with exponential backoff.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Available on Farm and Dairy plans.</p>
        </article>

        <article id="self-host">
            <h2>Self-hosting</h2>
            <p>
                GoatLab is open source. The same code that runs goatlab.app runs on your own
                server. Two-file install:
            </p>
            <pre><code>mkdir goatlab &amp;&amp; cd goatlab
curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chrisdfennell/GoatLabSaaS/main/docker-compose.oss.yml
curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chrisdfennell/GoatLabSaaS/main/.env.example
docker compose -f docker-compose.oss.yml up -d</code></pre>
            <p>
                Source: <MudLink Href="https://github.com/chrisdfennell/GoatLabSaaS" Target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/chrisdfennell/GoatLabSaaS</MudLink>.
                Image: <MudLink Href="https://hub.docker.com/r/fennch/goatlab" Target="_blank" rel="noopener">hub.docker.com/r/fennch/goatlab</MudLink>.
                In self-host mode, every feature is on for every tenant — no plan tiers, no
                Stripe/marketplace surface (unless you flip <code>Saas:Enabled=true</code>).
            </p>
        </article>

        <p class="help-footer">
            Still stuck? Open an issue at
            <MudLink Href="https://github.com/chrisdfennell/GoatLabSaaS/issues" Target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/chrisdfennell/GoatLabSaaS/issues</MudLink>
            — same place we triage bugs. Faster than email.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

